Here's a sample Java code that handles server crashes and prevents data loss:
```
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;
public class ServerCrashHandler {
    private static final String BACKUP_FILE = "server_backup.txt";
    private static final String CRASH_FILE = "server_crash.txt";

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            // Check if backup file exists
            File backupFile = new File(BACKUP_FILE);
            if (!backupFile.exists()) {
                System.out.println("Backup file does not exist. Creating new backup file...");
                backupServerData();
            }

            // Check if crash file exists
            File crashFile = new File(CRASH_FILE);
            if (crashFile.exists()) {
                System.out.println("Server crashed. Restoring previous state...");
                restoreServerData();
                crashFile.delete();
            }

            // Normal server operation
            while (true) {
                // Server code goes here
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Server crashed. Saving current state...");
            saveServerData();
            File crashFile = new File(CRASH_FILE);
            try {
                crashFile.createNewFile();
            } catch (IOException ex) {
                System.out.println("Error creating crash file.");
            }
        }
    }

    private static void backupServerData() {
        try {
            // Server data backup code goes here
            System.out.println("Server data backed up.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error backing up server data.");
        }
    }

    private static void restoreServerData() {
        try {
            // Server data restoration code goes here
            System.out.println("Server data restored.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error restoring server data.");
        }
    }

    private static void saveServerData() {
        try {
            // Server data saving code goes here
            System.out.println("Server data saved.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Error saving server data.");
        }
    }
}
```
The code checks if the backup file exists and creates a new one if it doesn't. It also checks if a crash file exists and restores the server to its previous state if it does. In the event of a server crash, the code saves the current state of the server and creates a crash file. 
