```javascript
function reverseSentences(inputString) {
  // Remove any punctuation or special characters from the string
  const cleanedString = inputString.replace(/[^\w\s]/gi, '');

  // Split the string into an array of sentences
  const sentences = cleanedString.split(/\.\s*/);

  // Reverse the order of words in each sentence
  const reversedSentences = sentences.map(sentence => {
    const words = sentence.split(/\s+/);
    return words.reverse().join(' ');
  });

  // Create a table to display the original and reversed sentences
  const table = [['Original Sentence', 'Reversed Sentence', 'Number of Words']];
  for (let i = 0; i < sentences.length; i++) {
    const originalSentence = sentences[i];
    const reversedSentence = reversedSentences[i];
    const numWords = originalSentence.split(/\s+/).length;
    table.push([originalSentence, reversedSentence, numWords]);
  }

  // Print the original and reversed string, as well as the table
  console.log('Original string:');
  console.log(inputString);
  console.log('Reversed string:');
  console.log(reversedSentences.join('. ') + '.');
  console.table(table);
}
// Example usage
const inputString = 'This is a test. It has multiple sentences, some of which have special characters like é, ö, and ç. This is the final sentence.';
reverseSentences(inputString);
```
This program first removes any punctuation or special characters from the input string using a regular expression. It then splits the string into an array of sentences using a regular expression that matches periods followed by optional whitespace. For each sentence, it splits the sentence into an array of words using a regular expression that matches whitespace, reverses the order of the words in the array, and joins them back together with spaces. It then creates a table that displays the original sentence, the reversed sentence, and the number of words in the original sentence. Finally, it prints both the original and reversed strings, as well as the table, to the console.
