Certainly! Here's an example implementation of the binary search algorithm in R:
```
binary_search <- function(arr, target) {
  left <- 1
  right <- length(arr)

  while (left <= right) {
    mid <- floor((left + right) / 2)

    if (arr[mid] == target) {
      return(mid)
    } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
      left <- mid + 1
    } else {
      right <- mid - 1
    }
  }

  return(-1)
}
```
This function takes in two arguments: `arr`, which is the sorted vector of values to search through, and `target`, which is the value we're looking for. The function returns the index of the target value in the vector, or -1 if the target is not found.
To demonstrate the time complexity of this algorithm, we can create a large sorted vector and time how long it takes to search for a target value using both the binary search algorithm and a linear search algorithm. Here's an example:
```
# Create a sorted vector of 10 million values
arr <- sort(sample.int(1e7, 1e7))
# Time how long it takes to search for a target value using binary search
target <- 5000000
start_time <- Sys.time()
binary_search(arr, target)
end_time <- Sys.time()
binary_search_time <- end_time - start_time
# Time how long it takes to search for a target value using linear search
start_time <- Sys.time()
which(arr == target)
end_time <- Sys.time()
linear_search_time <- end_time - start_time
# Print the results
cat("Binary search time:", binary_search_time, "\n")
cat("Linear search time:", linear_search_time, "\n")
```
In this example, we're searching for the value `5000000` in a sorted vector of 10 million values. The binary search algorithm should be much faster than the linear search algorithm, since it has a time complexity of O(log n) compared to the linear search algorithm's O(n) time complexity.

