Challenge accepted! Here's a solution to add a gradient to the background of a Swift iOS application, using only basic arithmetic operations and no pre-existing libraries or functions:
```swift
// Define the gradient colors
let startColor = UIColor(red: 0.0, green: 0.0, blue: 0.0, alpha: 1.0) // black
let endColor = UIColor(red: 1.0, green: 1.0, blue: 1.0, alpha: 1.0) // white
// Define the size of the gradient
let gradientSize = UIScreen.main.bounds.size
// Create a new bitmap context to draw the gradient into
UIGraphicsBeginImageContextWithOptions(gradientSize, true, 1)
// Get the context
let context = UIGraphicsGetCurrentContext()!
// Define the start and end points of the gradient
let startPoint = CGPoint.zero
let endPoint = CGPoint(x: 0, y: gradientSize.height)
// Define the colors and locations of the gradient
let colors = [startColor.cgColor, endColor.cgColor]
let locations: [CGFloat] = [0.0, 1.0]
// Create the gradient object
let colorSpace = CGColorSpaceCreateDeviceRGB()
let gradient = CGGradient(colorsSpace: colorSpace, colors: colors as CFArray, locations: locations)!
// Draw the gradient into the context
context.drawLinearGradient(gradient, start: startPoint, end: endPoint, options: [])
// Get the image from the context
let gradientImage = UIGraphicsGetImageFromCurrentImageContext()!
// End the context
UIGraphicsEndImageContext()
// Set the background color of the view to the gradient image
view.backgroundColor = UIColor(patternImage: gradientImage)
```
This code creates a new bitmap context to draw the gradient into, then uses basic arithmetic operations to define the start and end points of the gradient, the colors and locations of the gradient, and the size of the gradient. It then creates a gradient object and draws it into the context using the `drawLinearGradient` function. Finally, it gets the image from the context and sets the background color of the view to the gradient image using the `UIColor(patternImage:)` function.

