Here's an example of how you could use a recursive function to solve the puzzle:
```
public static bool SolvePuzzle(int[] boxes, int[] rules, int index)
{
    if (index == boxes.Length)
    {
        // Check if all rules are satisfied
        for (int i = 0; i < rules.Length; i++)
        {
            if (rules[i] != 0)
            {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    // Try moving the box left
    rules[boxes[index]]--;
    bool result = SolvePuzzle(boxes, rules, index + 1);
    rules[boxes[index]]++;

    // Try moving the box right
    rules[boxes[index]]++;
    result |= SolvePuzzle(boxes, rules, index + 1);
    rules[boxes[index]]--;

    return result;
}
```
In this example, `boxes` is an array of box numbers (0 to N-1), and `rules` is an array of rule counts. The `index` parameter is used to keep track of which box we're currently considering. 
To incorporate randomization, you can shuffle the `boxes` array using a random number generator before calling the `SolvePuzzle` function. This will simulate the unpredictable nature of the puzzle. 
Here's an example of how you could shuffle the `boxes` array:
```
public static void Shuffle(int[] array)
{
    Random rng = new Random();
    int n = array.Length;
    while (n > 1)
    {
        n--;
        int k = rng.Next(n + 1);
        int temp = array[k];
        array[k] = array[n];
        array[n] = temp;
    }
}
```
You can call this function before calling `SolvePuzzle` to shuffle the `boxes` array. 
```
