Sure, here is the completed code for the puzzle:
```
import java.util.*;
public class Solution {
    public boolean chooseKFromList(int[] nums, int target, int k) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        return helper(0, target, k, new ArrayList<Integer>(), nums);
    }

    private boolean helper(int index, int target, int k, List<Integer> list, int[] nums) {
        if (k == 1) {
            return Arrays.binarySearch(nums, target) >= 0;
        }
        for (int i = index; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (i > index && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) {
                continue;
            }
            if (nums[i] > target) {
                return false;
            }
            list.add(nums[i]);
            if (helper(i+1, target-nums[i], k-1, list, nums)) {
                return true;
            }
            list.remove(list.size()-1);
        }
        return false;
    }
}
```
The helper function uses recursion to check if it is possible to choose exactly k integers from nums that add up to target. It iterates over the list starting from index i until the end of the list and checks if it is possible to choose k-1 integers from the remaining list that add up to target-nums[i]. If it is possible, it returns true. Otherwise, it removes nums[i] from the list and continues with the next element. If no solution is found, it returns false. The function also handles duplicate elements in the list to avoid duplicate solutions.

