Here's the modified Java program that sorts tasks in ascending order of priority and filters out tasks with priority less than or equal to a given threshold:
```
import java.util.*;
public class TaskSorter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashMap<String, Integer> tasks = new HashMap<String, Integer>();
        tasks.put("Task 1", 2);
        tasks.put("Task 2", 1);
        tasks.put("Task 3", 3);

        int threshold = 2;

        List<String> filteredTasks = new ArrayList<String>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : tasks.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() > threshold) {
                filteredTasks.add(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        Collections.sort(filteredTasks, new Comparator<String>() {
            public int compare(String task1, String task2) {
                return tasks.get(task1) - tasks.get(task2);
            }
        });

        System.out.println(filteredTasks);
    }
}
```
To also include the ability to sort tasks in descending order of priority for those tasks meeting the threshold criteria, we can add another parameter to the program that indicates the sorting order. Here's the modified program:
```
import java.util.*;
public class TaskSorter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        HashMap<String, Integer> tasks = new HashMap<String, Integer>();
        tasks.put("Task 1", 2);
        tasks.put("Task 2", 1);
        tasks.put("Task 3", 3);

        int threshold = 2;
        String sortOrder = "ascending"; // or "descending"

        List<String> filteredTasks = new ArrayList<String>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : tasks.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() > threshold) {
                filteredTasks.add(entry.getKey());
            }
        }

        if (sortOrder.equals("ascending")) {
            Collections.sort(filteredTasks, new Comparator<String>() {
                public int compare(String task1, String task2) {
                    return tasks.get(task1) - tasks.get(task2);
                }
            });
        } else if (sortOrder.equals("descending")) {
            Collections.sort(filteredTasks, new Comparator<String>() {
                public int compare(String task1, String task2) {
                    return tasks.get(task2) - tasks.get(task1);
                }
            });
        }

        System.out.println(filteredTasks);
    }
}
```
This program now takes an additional input parameter `sortOrder` which can be set to either "ascending" or "descending". If set to "ascending", the tasks will be sorted in ascending order of priority, and if set to "descending", the tasks will be sorted in descending order of priority.

