Sure, I can help you create a MATLAB code that generates a weekly cleaning schedule that meets all the conditions you mentioned.
To do this, we can define the tasks that need to be done and the days of the week. We can also define the constraints that certain tasks cannot be assigned on the same day.
Here's the MATLAB code that generates a weekly cleaning schedule:
```matlab
% Define the tasks and days of the week
tasks = {'clean restrooms', 'vacuum floors', 'mop floors', 'dust furniture', 'clean windows'};
days = {'Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday'};
% Define the constraints
constraints = {'clean restrooms', 'mop floors'; 'vacuum floors', 'mop floors'};
% Generate a random permutation of the tasks
task_permutation = randperm(length(tasks));
% Initialize the schedule table
schedule = cell(length(days), length(tasks));
% Loop through each day of the week
for day_idx = 1:length(days)
    % Get the tasks that are not in conflict with the previous day
    if day_idx == 1
        available_tasks = tasks(task_permutation);
    else
        previous_day_tasks = schedule(day_idx-1,:);
        available_tasks = setdiff(tasks(task_permutation), previous_day_tasks);
        for conflict_idx = 1:size(constraints, 1)
            task1 = constraints{conflict_idx, 1};
            task2 = constraints{conflict_idx, 2};
            if ismember(task1, previous_day_tasks) && ismember(task2, available_tasks)
                available_tasks = setdiff(available_tasks, task2);
            elseif ismember(task2, previous_day_tasks) && ismember(task1, available_tasks)
                available_tasks = setdiff(available_tasks, task1);
            end
        end
    end

    % Assign a task to the current day
    task_idx = randi(length(available_tasks));
    schedule(day_idx, task_permutation(task_idx)) = {available_tasks{task_idx}};
end
% Display the schedule
disp(['Cleaning Schedule for the Week:']);
disp(days);
disp(schedule);
```
This code defines the tasks and days of the week, as well as the constraints that certain tasks cannot be assigned on the same day. It then generates a random permutation of the tasks and loops through each day of the week, assigning a task that is not in conflict with the previous day's tasks. The code then displays the final schedule.
